WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.461
The examples use a small synthetic healthcare
database The patients table has

00:00:05.487 --> 00:00:10.908
one row per patient Admissions lab results and
prescriptions are child tables

00:00:11.105 --> 00:00:14.316
so one patient can have zero one or many rows in
each

00:00:14.342 --> 00:00:19.863
of those tables Medical staff belongs to departments
and admissions

00:00:19.876 --> 00:00:24.589
can point to an attending staff member This gives us
several realistic

00:00:24.602 --> 00:00:30.729
join paths The data also includes useful edge cases
At

00:00:30.756 --> 00:00:34.335
least one patient has no admission or lab activity
and the staging

00:00:34.361 --> 00:00:37.624
table contains a patient ID that does not exist in
the patient

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:44.261
master table Those records help us see what outer
joins preserve Keep

00:00:44.287 --> 00:00:49.118
the relationships in mind while reading every query
If the patients table

00:00:49.145 --> 00:00:53.053
contains one row per patient but admissions contains
many rows per patient

00:00:53.316 --> 00:00:57.776
a patient-to-admission join naturally changes the
grain from patient to admission detail

00:00:58.711 --> 00:01:03.605
That is not automatically a duplicate problem It is
a consequence of

00:01:03.632 --> 00:01:04.579
the relationship
